<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cantona</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 1050 CE (approx.) · capital Cantona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A huge fortified city in the eastern Puebla highlands, notable for its many ballcourts and its control of the Oyameles-Zaragoza obsidian source.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cantona grew from about 600 BCE on a rough lava field in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puebla-state">Puebla</a>. Its position near the Oyameles-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> obsidian deposits and on routes between the central highlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> made it an important manufacturing and trading centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was densely built of unmortared stone, with walled residential compounds, raised causeways and controlled entrances that suggest strong concern with defence. It has more than twenty ballcourts, the most known from any Mesoamerican site, pointing to the political and ritual importance of the game. Obsidian tools were produced on a large scale. Cantona seems to have remained independent of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cantona reached its height after Teotihuacan's decline, between about 600 and 900 CE, then lost population and was abandoned by about 1050. Rediscovered in modern times, it was opened to visitors in 1994.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 600 BCE – 1050 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.76%;width:32.84%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 1050 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-centur